Q(14,1,1,"On a commercial vehicle, what is the minimum tread depth for front tires?","4/32 inch in every major groove",["2/32 inch", "1/32 inch", "6/32 inch"],"Front tires need at least 4/32-inch tread in every major groove. Other tires need 2/32 inch.");
F(14,1,"On a commercial vehicle, what is the minimum tread depth for front tires?","4/32 inch in every major groove. Front tires need at least 4/32-inch tread in every major groove. Other tires need 2/32 inch.");
Q(14,1,1,"What is the minimum tread depth for tires other than the front tires?","2/32 inch",["4/32 inch", "1/32 inch", "8/32 inch"],"Tires other than front tires need at least 2/32 inch. No fabric should show through the tread or sidewall.");
F(14,1,"What is the minimum tread depth for tires other than the front tires?","2/32 inch. Tires other than front tires need at least 2/32 inch. No fabric should show through the tread or sidewall.");
Q(14,1,1,"Steering wheel play of more than how many degrees can make it hard to steer?","10 degrees",["2 degrees", "5 degrees", "30 degrees"],"More than 10 degrees of play, about 2 inches at the rim of a 20-inch wheel, can make steering hard.");
F(14,1,"Steering wheel play of more than how many degrees can make it hard to steer?","10 degrees. More than 10 degrees of play, about 2 inches at the rim of a 20-inch wheel, can make steering hard.");
Q(14,1,1,"A vehicle must be put out of service if what share of the leaves in a leaf spring are missing or broken?","One-fourth or more",["One-tenth", "Any single leaf", "One-half or more"],"Missing or broken leaves are a suspension defect. If one-fourth or more are missing, the vehicle is out of service.");
F(14,1,"A vehicle must be put out of service if what share of the leaves in a leaf spring are missing or broken?","One-fourth or more. Missing or broken leaves are a suspension defect. If one-fourth or more are missing, the vehicle is out of service.");
Q(14,1,1,"Which emergency equipment must a commercial vehicle carry?","Fire extinguisher, spare electrical fuses (unless it has circuit breakers) and warning devices for parked vehicles",["A spare tire only", "A first aid kit only", "Tire chains only"],"Vehicles must have a fire extinguisher, spare fuses unless equipped with circuit breakers, and warning devices such as three reflective triangles.");
F(14,1,"Which emergency equipment must a commercial vehicle carry?","Fire extinguisher, spare electrical fuses (unless it has circuit breakers) and warning devices for parked vehicles. Vehicles must have a fire extinguisher, spare fuses unless equipped with circuit breakers, and warning devices such as three reflective triangles.");
Q(14,1,1,"Why should you do a vehicle inspection before every trip?","Safety is the most important reason, and federal and state laws require it",["Only because the company asks", "Only to save fuel", "Only for long trips"],"Inspecting finds defects that could cause a crash or breakdown, and laws require drivers to inspect their vehicles.");
F(14,1,"Why should you do a vehicle inspection before every trip?","Safety is the most important reason, and federal and state laws require it. Inspecting finds defects that could cause a crash or breakdown, and laws require drivers to inspect their vehicles.");
Q(14,1,1,"During a trip, how soon after starting should you check cargo securement?","Within the first 50 miles",["Within the first 10 miles", "After 200 miles", "Only at the destination"],"Inspect the cargo and its securing devices within the first 50 miles after beginning a trip.");
F(14,1,"During a trip, how soon after starting should you check cargo securement?","Within the first 50 miles. Inspect the cargo and its securing devices within the first 50 miles after beginning a trip.");
Q(14,1,1,"After the first check, how often must you re-check cargo and securing devices?","Every 3 hours or 150 miles",["Every 8 hours", "Every 500 miles", "Once a day"],"Re-check after you have driven 3 hours or 150 miles, and after every break.");
F(14,1,"After the first check, how often must you re-check cargo and securing devices?","Every 3 hours or 150 miles. Re-check after you have driven 3 hours or 150 miles, and after every break.");
Q(14,1,1,"When backing a commercial vehicle, which direction is safest to back toward?","The driver's side",["The right side", "Whichever is closer", "Straight back only"],"Back and turn toward the driver's side so you can see better. Use a helper when you can.");
F(14,1,"When backing a commercial vehicle, which direction is safest to back toward?","The driver's side. Back and turn toward the driver's side so you can see better. Use a helper when you can.");
Q(14,1,1,"What is the proper way to hold the steering wheel?","Firmly with both hands on opposite sides of the wheel",["With one hand at the top", "With fingertips only", "With one hand at the bottom"],"Hold the wheel firmly with both hands on opposite sides. If you hit a curb or pothole, the wheel could pull away from you.");
F(14,1,"What is the proper way to hold the steering wheel?","Firmly with both hands on opposite sides of the wheel. Hold the wheel firmly with both hands on opposite sides. If you hit a curb or pothole, the wheel could pull away from you.");
Q(14,1,1,"What does \"double clutching\" involve when upshifting a manual transmission?","Release accelerator, clutch in and shift to neutral, release clutch, let rpm drop, clutch in and shift up",["Pressing the clutch twice quickly without shifting", "Shifting without the clutch", "Holding the clutch down the whole time"],"Most heavy vehicles with unsynchronized manual transmissions need double clutching to change gears.");
F(14,1,"What does \"double clutching\" involve when upshifting a manual transmission?","Release accelerator, clutch in and shift to neutral, release clutch, let rpm drop, clutch in and shift up. Most heavy vehicles with unsynchronized manual transmissions need double clutching to change gears.");
Q(14,1,1,"Retarders help slow a vehicle. When should you turn the retarder off?","When the road is wet, icy or snow covered",["On dry downgrades", "On the interstate", "At night"],"When your drive wheels have poor traction, the retarder may cause them to skid. Turn it off on wet, icy or snow-covered roads.");
F(14,1,"Retarders help slow a vehicle. When should you turn the retarder off?","When the road is wet, icy or snow covered. When your drive wheels have poor traction, the retarder may cause them to skid. Turn it off on wet, icy or snow-covered roads.");
Q(14,2,1,"How far ahead should a commercial driver look while driving?","12 to 15 seconds",["3 to 5 seconds", "1 to 2 seconds", "30 to 40 seconds"],"Good drivers look 12 to 15 seconds ahead. At lower speeds that is about one block and at highway speeds about a quarter of a mile.");
F(14,2,"How far ahead should a commercial driver look while driving?","12 to 15 seconds. Good drivers look 12 to 15 seconds ahead. At lower speeds that is about one block and at highway speeds about a quarter of a mile.");
Q(14,2,1,"At speeds below 40 mph, how much following distance do you need?","One second for each 10 feet of vehicle length",["One second total", "One car length", "Two seconds for any vehicle"],"You need at least one second for each 10 feet of vehicle length below 40 mph, and one more second above 40 mph.");
F(14,2,"At speeds below 40 mph, how much following distance do you need?","One second for each 10 feet of vehicle length. You need at least one second for each 10 feet of vehicle length below 40 mph, and one more second above 40 mph.");
Q(14,2,1,"You are driving a 40-foot vehicle at 50 mph. How many seconds of following distance should you keep?","5 seconds",["2 seconds", "3 seconds", "4 seconds"],"Four seconds for 40 feet of length plus one more second because your speed is over 40 mph.");
F(14,2,"You are driving a 40-foot vehicle at 50 mph. How many seconds of following distance should you keep?","5 seconds. Four seconds for 40 feet of length plus one more second because your speed is over 40 mph.");
Q(14,2,1,"At 55 mph on dry pavement, about how far does it take to stop a vehicle with good brakes?","About 419 feet",["About 100 feet", "About 200 feet", "About 800 feet"],"Perception distance, reaction distance and braking distance add up to about 419 feet at 55 mph.");
F(14,2,"At 55 mph on dry pavement, about how far does it take to stop a vehicle with good brakes?","About 419 feet. Perception distance, reaction distance and braking distance add up to about 419 feet at 55 mph.");
Q(14,2,1,"On a wet road, by how much should you reduce your speed?","About one-third",["About one-tenth", "Not at all", "By three-quarters"],"Wet roads can double stopping distance. Reduce speed by about one-third, for example from 55 to about 35 mph.");
F(14,2,"On a wet road, by how much should you reduce your speed?","About one-third. Wet roads can double stopping distance. Reduce speed by about one-third, for example from 55 to about 35 mph.");
Q(14,2,1,"On packed snow, by how much should you reduce your speed?","One-half or more",["One-tenth", "One-quarter", "Not at all"],"Reduce speed by half or more on packed snow. On ice, slow to a crawl and stop driving as soon as you safely can.");
F(14,2,"On packed snow, by how much should you reduce your speed?","One-half or more. Reduce speed by half or more on packed snow. On ice, slow to a crawl and stop driving as soon as you safely can.");
Q(14,2,1,"Hydroplaning can begin at speeds as low as","30 mph",["10 mph", "50 mph", "65 mph"],"If there is a lot of water, hydroplaning can occur at speeds as low as 30 mph. It is more likely with low tire pressure or worn tread.");
F(14,2,"Hydroplaning can begin at speeds as low as","30 mph. If there is a lot of water, hydroplaning can occur at speeds as low as 30 mph. It is more likely with low tire pressure or worn tread.");
Q(14,2,1,"With low beams, about how far ahead can you see?","About 250 feet",["About 50 feet", "About 500 feet", "About 1,000 feet"],"Low beams let you see about 250 feet and high beams about 350 to 500 feet. Adjust speed so you can stop within the distance you can see.");
F(14,2,"With low beams, about how far ahead can you see?","About 250 feet. Low beams let you see about 250 feet and high beams about 350 to 500 feet. Adjust speed so you can stop within the distance you can see.");
Q(14,2,1,"When must you dim your headlights?","Within 500 feet of an oncoming vehicle and when following within 500 feet",["Only in cities", "Within 100 feet of any vehicle", "Never on highways"],"Glare from your lights can blind other drivers. Dim within 500 feet of an oncoming vehicle and when following another vehicle within 500 feet.");
F(14,2,"When must you dim your headlights?","Within 500 feet of an oncoming vehicle and when following within 500 feet. Glare from your lights can blind other drivers. Dim within 500 feet of an oncoming vehicle and when following another vehicle within 500 feet.");
Q(14,2,1,"What do convex (curved) mirrors do?","Show a wider area, but make things look smaller and farther away than they are",["Make objects look larger", "Show a narrower view", "Remove all blind spots"],"Convex mirrors show a wider area than flat mirrors, but everything appears smaller and farther away than it really is.");
F(14,2,"What do convex (curved) mirrors do?","Show a wider area, but make things look smaller and farther away than they are. Convex mirrors show a wider area than flat mirrors, but everything appears smaller and farther away than it really is.");
Q(14,2,1,"If you must stop on the shoulder of a road, when must you put out your emergency warning devices?","Within ten minutes",["Within one hour", "Within thirty minutes", "Only at night"],"If you must stop on a road or shoulder, put out your emergency warning devices within ten minutes.");
F(14,2,"If you must stop on the shoulder of a road, when must you put out your emergency warning devices?","Within ten minutes. If you must stop on a road or shoulder, put out your emergency warning devices within ten minutes.");
Q(14,2,1,"On a two-lane road with traffic in both directions, where do you place warning devices when stopped?","Within 10 feet of the front or rear corners, and 100 feet behind and ahead of the vehicle",["All three right behind the vehicle", "500 feet behind only", "On the roof"],"Place devices within 10 feet of the front or rear corners to mark the vehicle's location, and 100 feet behind and ahead on the shoulder or in your lane.");
F(14,2,"On a two-lane road with traffic in both directions, where do you place warning devices when stopped?","Within 10 feet of the front or rear corners, and 100 feet behind and ahead of the vehicle. Place devices within 10 feet of the front or rear corners to mark the vehicle's location, and 100 feet behind and ahead on the shoulder or in your lane.");
Q(14,2,1,"Which is the correct way to make a right turn in a long vehicle when you cannot stay in your lane?","Turn wide as you complete the turn, keeping the rear close to the curb",["Swing wide to the left before starting the turn", "Back up and try again", "Turn from the left lane"],"Do not turn wide to the left as you start the turn, because a following driver may try to pass on the right. Turn wide as you complete the turn.");
F(14,2,"Which is the correct way to make a right turn in a long vehicle when you cannot stay in your lane?","Turn wide as you complete the turn, keeping the rear close to the curb. Do not turn wide to the left as you start the turn, because a following driver may try to pass on the right. Turn wide as you complete the turn.");
Q(14,2,1,"Which statement about managing space is true?","The area ahead of the vehicle is the most important space",["Space behind matters most", "Space matters only on highways", "Smaller vehicles need more space than large ones"],"Of all the space around your vehicle, the area ahead, the space you are driving into, is the most important.");
F(14,2,"Which statement about managing space is true?","The area ahead of the vehicle is the most important space. Of all the space around your vehicle, the area ahead, the space you are driving into, is the most important.");
Q(14,2,1,"What should you do if you are being tailgated?","Avoid quick changes and increase your following distance",["Speed up", "Flash your brake lights repeatedly", "Brake hard"],"Avoid quick changes, increase your following distance and do not speed up. Opening room ahead helps you avoid sudden braking.");
F(14,2,"What should you do if you are being tailgated?","Avoid quick changes and increase your following distance. Avoid quick changes, increase your following distance and do not speed up. Opening room ahead helps you avoid sudden braking.");
Q(14,2,1,"What is the proper braking technique on a long, steep downgrade once you are in the proper low gear?","Brake until you are about 5 mph below your safe speed, release, and repeat when you reach the safe speed again",["Hold light steady pressure all the way down", "Pump the brakes rapidly", "Shift to neutral and coast"],"Apply the brakes hard enough to feel a definite slowdown, release at about 5 mph below your safe speed, and repeat.");
F(14,2,"What is the proper braking technique on a long, steep downgrade once you are in the proper low gear?","Brake until you are about 5 mph below your safe speed, release, and repeat when you reach the safe speed again. Apply the brakes hard enough to feel a definite slowdown, release at about 5 mph below your safe speed, and repeat.");
Q(14,2,1,"What is the purpose of an escape ramp?","To stop runaway vehicles safely without injuring drivers and passengers",["To let trucks pass", "To rest tired drivers", "To weigh vehicles"],"Escape ramps use a long bed of loose soft material or an upgrade to stop a runaway vehicle. Use one if your brakes fail on a downgrade.");
F(14,2,"What is the purpose of an escape ramp?","To stop runaway vehicles safely without injuring drivers and passengers. Escape ramps use a long bed of loose soft material or an upgrade to stop a runaway vehicle. Use one if your brakes fail on a downgrade.");
Q(14,2,1,"At a railroad crossing where you must stop, how far from the nearest rail should you stop?","No closer than 15 feet and no farther than 50 feet",["Within 5 feet", "100 feet or more", "Exactly 200 feet"],"A full stop is required no closer than 15 feet and no farther than 50 feet from the nearest rail.");
F(14,2,"At a railroad crossing where you must stop, how far from the nearest rail should you stop?","No closer than 15 feet and no farther than 50 feet. A full stop is required no closer than 15 feet and no farther than 50 feet from the nearest rail.");
Q(14,3,1,"In an emergency, what is often quicker than stopping to avoid an obstacle?","Steering to miss it",["Braking as hard as possible", "Sounding the horn", "Shifting to neutral"],"You can almost always turn to miss an obstacle more quickly than you can stop. Stopping is not always the safest thing to do.");
F(14,3,"In an emergency, what is often quicker than stopping to avoid an obstacle?","Steering to miss it. You can almost always turn to miss an obstacle more quickly than you can stop. Stopping is not always the safest thing to do.");
Q(14,3,1,"What should you do if a front tire fails?","Hold the steering wheel firmly and stay off the brake until the vehicle has slowed",["Brake hard immediately", "Steer sharply to the shoulder", "Speed up"],"Hold the wheel firmly, stay off the brake, and after the vehicle has slowed, pull off and stop.");
F(14,3,"What should you do if a front tire fails?","Hold the steering wheel firmly and stay off the brake until the vehicle has slowed. Hold the wheel firmly, stay off the brake, and after the vehicle has slowed, pull off and stop.");
Q(14,3,1,"If your hydraulic brakes fail, what can you do?","Downshift, pump the brakes and use the parking brake",["Turn off the engine", "Shift to neutral", "Open the door"],"Shift down, pump the pedal to build pressure, and use the parking or emergency brake while holding the release.");
F(14,3,"If your hydraulic brakes fail, what can you do?","Downshift, pump the brakes and use the parking brake. Shift down, pump the pedal to build pressure, and use the parking or emergency brake while holding the release.");
Q(14,3,1,"What is the most common cause of a skid?","Rear wheels losing traction through excessive braking or acceleration",["Brake failure", "Low tire pressure alone", "Turning too slowly"],"By far the most common skid is one where the rear wheels lose traction through over-braking or over-accelerating.");
F(14,3,"What is the most common cause of a skid?","Rear wheels losing traction through excessive braking or acceleration. By far the most common skid is one where the rear wheels lose traction through over-braking or over-accelerating.");
Q(14,3,1,"How do you correct a drive-wheel braking skid?","Stop braking, turn quickly and counter-steer",["Brake harder", "Accelerate", "Hold the wheel straight"],"Stop braking so the rear wheels can roll again, steer in the direction you want to go, and countersteer as the vehicle comes back.");
F(14,3,"How do you correct a drive-wheel braking skid?","Stop braking, turn quickly and counter-steer. Stop braking so the rear wheels can roll again, steer in the direction you want to go, and countersteer as the vehicle comes back.");
Q(14,3,1,"Which type of fire extinguisher is designed for electrical fires and burning liquids?","B:C",["Water", "A only", "D only"],"The B:C type works on electrical fires and burning liquids. The A:B:C type also works on burning wood, paper and cloth.");
F(14,3,"Which type of fire extinguisher is designed for electrical fires and burning liquids?","B:C. The B:C type works on electrical fires and burning liquids. The A:B:C type also works on burning wood, paper and cloth.");
Q(14,3,1,"Should you use water on a gasoline or electrical fire?","No",["Yes, always", "Yes, on gasoline only", "Yes, on electrical only"],"Water can spread a gasoline fire and can cause shock on an electrical fire. Use water on wood, paper or cloth.");
F(14,3,"Should you use water on a gasoline or electrical fire?","No. Water can spread a gasoline fire and can cause shock on an electrical fire. Use water on wood, paper or cloth.");
Q(14,3,1,"At what blood alcohol concentration is a commercial driver considered to be driving under the influence?","0.04% or more",["0.08% or more", "0.10% or more", "0.02% or more"],"If your BAC is 0.04% or more while operating a commercial motor vehicle, you are driving under the influence.");
F(14,3,"At what blood alcohol concentration is a commercial driver considered to be driving under the influence?","0.04% or more. If your BAC is 0.04% or more while operating a commercial motor vehicle, you are driving under the influence.");
Q(14,3,1,"A commercial driver with any detectable amount of alcohol under 0.04% is put out of service for","24 hours",["1 hour", "8 hours", "72 hours"],"Any detectable amount under 0.04% means you are placed out of service for 24 hours.");
F(14,3,"A commercial driver with any detectable amount of alcohol under 0.04% is put out of service for","24 hours. Any detectable amount under 0.04% means you are placed out of service for 24 hours.");
Q(14,3,1,"What is the first conviction penalty for driving a commercial vehicle with a BAC of 0.04% or higher?","Loss of your CDL for at least one year",["A warning", "A 30-day suspension", "Loss of your CDL for life"],"A first offense means losing your CDL for at least one year, or three years if you were hauling placarded hazardous materials.");
F(14,3,"What is the first conviction penalty for driving a commercial vehicle with a BAC of 0.04% or higher?","Loss of your CDL for at least one year. A first offense means losing your CDL for at least one year, or three years if you were hauling placarded hazardous materials.");
Q(14,3,1,"How many tie-downs does cargo on a flatbed need at minimum?","At least one for each ten feet of cargo, and at least two no matter how small the cargo",["One in total", "One for each 25 feet", "Three for any cargo"],"Cargo should have at least one tie-down for each ten feet, with at least two tie-downs whatever the size.");
F(14,3,"How many tie-downs does cargo on a flatbed need at minimum?","At least one for each ten feet of cargo, and at least two no matter how small the cargo. Cargo should have at least one tie-down for each ten feet, with at least two tie-downs whatever the size.");
Q(14,3,1,"What happens if too little weight is on the front (steering) axle?","The steering axle can be too light to steer safely",["The vehicle stops faster", "Fuel use drops", "Nothing changes"],"Under-loaded front axles, caused by shifting weight too far to the rear, can make the steering axle too light to steer safely.");
F(14,3,"What happens if too little weight is on the front (steering) axle?","The steering axle can be too light to steer safely. Under-loaded front axles, caused by shifting weight too far to the rear, can make the steering axle too light to steer safely.");
Q(14,3,1,"What shape are hazardous materials placards?","Diamond",["Circle", "Octagon", "Rectangle"],"Placards are diamond-shaped signs placed on the outside of a vehicle to identify the hazard class of the cargo.");
F(14,3,"What shape are hazardous materials placards?","Diamond. Placards are diamond-shaped signs placed on the outside of a vehicle to identify the hazard class of the cargo.");
Q(14,3,1,"What is a danger of cargo with a high center of gravity?","The vehicle is more likely to roll over",["It stops faster", "It handles better in curves", "Nothing"],"A high center of gravity means you are more likely to tip over, especially in curves or when swerving.");
F(14,3,"What is a danger of cargo with a high center of gravity?","The vehicle is more likely to roll over. A high center of gravity means you are more likely to tip over, especially in curves or when swerving.");
Q(14,3,1,"Texting while driving a commercial motor vehicle is","Prohibited",["Allowed at low speed", "Allowed with one hand", "Allowed on rural roads"],"Federal rules prohibit texting while driving a commercial motor vehicle, and using a hand-held mobile telephone.");
F(14,3,"Texting while driving a commercial motor vehicle is","Prohibited. Federal rules prohibit texting while driving a commercial motor vehicle, and using a hand-held mobile telephone.");
Q(14,3,1,"Which gear should you use going down a long downgrade?","A low gear, shifted before starting down",["Neutral", "The highest gear", "Whatever gear you were in"],"Be in the right low gear before starting down the grade. Do not try to downshift after your speed has already built up.");
F(14,3,"Which gear should you use going down a long downgrade?","A low gear, shifted before starting down. Be in the right low gear before starting down the grade. Do not try to downshift after your speed has already built up.");
Q(14,4,2,"In an air brake system, at about what pressure does the governor stop the compressor from pumping air?","Around 125 psi",["Around 60 psi", "Around 85 psi", "Around 200 psi"],"The governor cuts out the compressor at around 125 psi and cuts it back in at around 100 psi.");
F(14,4,"In an air brake system, at about what pressure does the governor stop the compressor from pumping air?","Around 125 psi. The governor cuts out the compressor at around 125 psi and cuts it back in at around 100 psi.");
Q(14,4,2,"At about what pressure does the governor start the compressor pumping again?","Around 100 psi",["Around 20 psi", "Around 60 psi", "Around 150 psi"],"The cut-in pressure is around 100 psi.");
F(14,4,"At about what pressure does the governor start the compressor pumping again?","Around 100 psi. The cut-in pressure is around 100 psi.");
Q(14,4,2,"The low air pressure warning signal must come on before pressure in the tanks falls below","55 psi",["100 psi", "80 psi", "20 psi"],"A warning signal you can see must come on before the air pressure in the tanks falls below 55 psi, or half the governor cut-out pressure on older vehicles.");
F(14,4,"The low air pressure warning signal must come on before pressure in the tanks falls below","55 psi. A warning signal you can see must come on before the air pressure in the tanks falls below 55 psi, or half the governor cut-out pressure on older vehicles.");
Q(14,4,2,"In a tractor-trailer, at what pressure range will the spring brakes come on automatically as air is lost?","20 to 45 psi",["60 to 80 psi", "90 to 100 psi", "5 to 10 psi"],"Tractor and straight truck spring brakes come fully on when air pressure drops to a range of 20 to 45 psi.");
F(14,4,"In a tractor-trailer, at what pressure range will the spring brakes come on automatically as air is lost?","20 to 45 psi. Tractor and straight truck spring brakes come fully on when air pressure drops to a range of 20 to 45 psi.");
Q(14,4,2,"The safety relief valve in an air tank is usually set to open at","150 psi",["60 psi", "100 psi", "200 psi"],"The safety valve is usually set to open at 150 psi. If it releases air, something is wrong and should be fixed.");
F(14,4,"The safety relief valve in an air tank is usually set to open at","150 psi. The safety valve is usually set to open at 150 psi. If it releases air, something is wrong and should be fixed.");
Q(14,4,2,"Why must air tanks be drained?","Compressed air contains water and compressor oil, which can freeze or cause brake failure",["To cool the tanks", "To raise pressure", "To clean the gauges"],"Water and oil collect in the tanks. Water can freeze in cold weather and cause brake failure, so tanks must be drained.");
F(14,4,"Why must air tanks be drained?","Compressed air contains water and compressor oil, which can freeze or cause brake failure. Water and oil collect in the tanks. Water can freeze in cold weather and cause brake failure, so tanks must be drained.");
Q(14,4,2,"With the engine off and the brakes released, the air pressure loss for a single vehicle should be less than","2 psi in one minute",["5 psi in one minute", "10 psi in one minute", "1 psi in ten minutes"],"The static leakage rate should be less than 2 psi in one minute for single vehicles and 3 psi for combination vehicles.");
F(14,4,"With the engine off and the brakes released, the air pressure loss for a single vehicle should be less than","2 psi in one minute. The static leakage rate should be less than 2 psi in one minute for single vehicles and 3 psi for combination vehicles.");
Q(14,4,2,"With the engine off and the brakes released, the air pressure loss for a combination vehicle should be less than","3 psi in one minute",["1 psi in one minute", "6 psi in one minute", "10 psi in one minute"],"The limit is 3 psi in one minute for combination vehicles.");
F(14,4,"With the engine off and the brakes released, the air pressure loss for a combination vehicle should be less than","3 psi in one minute. The limit is 3 psi in one minute for combination vehicles.");
Q(14,4,2,"With dual air systems, how fast should pressure build from 85 to 100 psi?","Within 45 seconds",["Within 5 minutes", "Within 3 minutes", "Within 10 seconds"],"In dual air systems, pressure should build from 85 to 100 psi within 45 seconds.");
F(14,4,"With dual air systems, how fast should pressure build from 85 to 100 psi?","Within 45 seconds. In dual air systems, pressure should build from 85 to 100 psi within 45 seconds.");
Q(14,4,2,"When checking a manual slack adjuster, how far should it move when you pull hard?","Not more than about one inch",["About three inches", "About six inches", "It should not move at all"],"If a slack adjuster moves more than about one inch where the push rod attaches, it probably needs adjustment.");
F(14,4,"When checking a manual slack adjuster, how far should it move when you pull hard?","Not more than about one inch. If a slack adjuster moves more than about one inch where the push rod attaches, it probably needs adjustment.");
Q(14,4,2,"Which type of foundation brake is the most common?","S-cam drum brakes",["Wedge brakes", "Disc brakes", "Hydraulic drum brakes"],"The S-cam brake is the most common type. Pushing the brake pedal turns the S-cam, which forces the shoes against the drum.");
F(14,4,"Which type of foundation brake is the most common?","S-cam drum brakes. The S-cam brake is the most common type. Pushing the brake pedal turns the S-cam, which forces the shoes against the drum.");
Q(14,4,2,"What is brake lag in an air brake system?","The time it takes air to flow through the lines to the brakes after you press the pedal",["The time brakes take to cool", "The wear on the linings", "The delay of the parking brake"],"Air brakes take about half a second or more to work after the pedal is pushed, which adds to stopping distance.");
F(14,4,"What is brake lag in an air brake system?","The time it takes air to flow through the lines to the brakes after you press the pedal. Air brakes take about half a second or more to work after the pedal is pushed, which adds to stopping distance.");
Q(14,4,2,"At 55 mph on dry pavement, how much distance does air brake lag add?","About 32 feet",["About 5 feet", "About 100 feet", "About 200 feet"],"Brake lag adds about 32 feet at 55 mph, for a total stopping distance of over 450 feet.");
F(14,4,"At 55 mph on dry pavement, how much distance does air brake lag add?","About 32 feet. Brake lag adds about 32 feet at 55 mph, for a total stopping distance of over 450 feet.");
Q(14,4,2,"What colors and shape is the parking brake control knob on newer vehicles?","A yellow, diamond-shaped, push-pull knob",["A red round button", "A blue lever", "A green switch"],"In newer vehicles you set the parking brakes with a diamond-shaped, yellow, push-pull control knob.");
F(14,4,"What colors and shape is the parking brake control knob on newer vehicles?","A yellow, diamond-shaped, push-pull knob. In newer vehicles you set the parking brakes with a diamond-shaped, yellow, push-pull control knob.");
Q(14,4,2,"When should you not use the parking brakes?","When the brakes are very hot, or when they are very wet in freezing temperatures",["Whenever you park on a hill", "Whenever the engine is off", "When parked for more than an hour"],"Hot brakes can be damaged by the heat, and wet brakes can freeze so the vehicle cannot move. Use wheel chocks instead.");
F(14,4,"When should you not use the parking brakes?","When the brakes are very hot, or when they are very wet in freezing temperatures. Hot brakes can be damaged by the heat, and wet brakes can freeze so the vehicle cannot move. Use wheel chocks instead.");
Q(14,4,2,"What is the yellow ABS malfunction lamp for?","It tells you if something is not working in the anti-lock brake system",["It shows low air pressure", "It shows the parking brake is on", "It shows high coolant temperature"],"Tractors, trucks and buses have yellow ABS malfunction lamps on the instrument panel.");
F(14,4,"What is the yellow ABS malfunction lamp for?","It tells you if something is not working in the anti-lock brake system. Tractors, trucks and buses have yellow ABS malfunction lamps on the instrument panel.");
Q(14,4,2,"What does an anti-lock braking system (ABS) do?","Keeps the wheels from locking up during hard braking",["Shortens the stopping distance in every case", "Replaces the normal brakes", "Increases air pressure"],"ABS helps you keep control by preventing wheel lockup. It does not necessarily shorten stopping distance.");
F(14,4,"What does an anti-lock braking system (ABS) do?","Keeps the wheels from locking up during hard braking. ABS helps you keep control by preventing wheel lockup. It does not necessarily shorten stopping distance.");
Q(14,4,2,"What is \"controlled braking\"?","Applying the brakes as hard as you can without locking the wheels",["Pumping the brakes rapidly", "Braking only with the trailer brake", "Using the parking brake"],"Apply the brakes as hard as you can without locking the wheels and keep steering movements very small.");
F(14,4,"What is \"controlled braking\"?","Applying the brakes as hard as you can without locking the wheels. Apply the brakes as hard as you can without locking the wheels and keep steering movements very small.");
Q(14,4,2,"What is \"stab braking\"?","Apply the brakes fully, release when the wheels lock up, and reapply as soon as they start rolling",["Apply light steady pressure", "Use the hand valve only", "Use the retarder"],"Stab braking is for vehicles without ABS. Brake fully, release when wheels lock, and brake again when they roll.");
F(14,4,"What is \"stab braking\"?","Apply the brakes fully, release when the wheels lock up, and reapply as soon as they start rolling. Stab braking is for vehicles without ABS. Brake fully, release when wheels lock, and brake again when they roll.");
Q(14,4,2,"What does the alcohol evaporator do in some air brake systems?","Helps reduce the risk of ice in air brake valves in cold weather",["Cleans the brake linings", "Raises air pressure", "Cools the compressor"],"An alcohol evaporator puts alcohol into the air system to reduce the risk of ice in valves during cold weather.");
F(14,4,"What does the alcohol evaporator do in some air brake systems?","Helps reduce the risk of ice in air brake valves in cold weather. An alcohol evaporator puts alcohol into the air system to reduce the risk of ice in valves during cold weather.");
Q(14,4,2,"What is a dual air brake system?","Two separate air brake systems that use a single set of brake controls",["Air brakes plus hydraulic brakes", "Two brake pedals", "Two parking brakes"],"Most heavy-duty vehicles use dual systems, called primary and secondary, each with its own tanks, hoses and lines.");
F(14,4,"What is a dual air brake system?","Two separate air brake systems that use a single set of brake controls. Most heavy-duty vehicles use dual systems, called primary and secondary, each with its own tanks, hoses and lines.");
Q(14,4,2,"How do you test the service brakes?","Move forward slowly at about five mph and apply the brake pedal firmly",["Brake hard at highway speed", "Pull the parking brake while moving", "Listen for leaks only"],"Wait for normal air pressure, release the parking brake, move forward at about five mph and apply the brakes firmly.");
F(14,4,"How do you test the service brakes?","Move forward slowly at about five mph and apply the brake pedal firmly. Wait for normal air pressure, release the parking brake, move forward at about five mph and apply the brakes firmly.");
Q(14,5,2,"Compared with empty rigs, fully loaded rigs are how much more likely to roll over in a crash?","Ten times more likely",["Twice as likely", "Equally likely", "Less likely"],"Fully loaded rigs are 10 times more likely to roll over in a crash than empty rigs.");
F(14,5,"Compared with empty rigs, fully loaded rigs are how much more likely to roll over in a crash?","Ten times more likely. Fully loaded rigs are 10 times more likely to roll over in a crash than empty rigs.");
Q(14,5,2,"What is \"rearward amplification\"?","The crack-the-whip effect that can turn the last trailer over during a quick lane change",["Louder brake noise at the rear", "Extra traction on the rear axle", "A longer stopping distance"],"Trucks with trailers have a dangerous crack-the-whip effect. A quick lane change can tip the rear trailer.");
F(14,5,"What is \"rearward amplification\"?","The crack-the-whip effect that can turn the last trailer over during a quick lane change. Trucks with trailers have a dangerous crack-the-whip effect. A quick lane change can tip the rear trailer.");
Q(14,5,2,"Which stops more quickly, a loaded tractor-trailer or a bobtail tractor?","A bobtail tractor can take longer to stop than a loaded tractor-trailer",["The bobtail always stops faster", "They stop the same", "It depends only on the driver"],"Bobtail tractors can be very hard to stop smoothly and take longer to stop than a tractor-semitrailer loaded to gross weight.");
F(14,5,"Which stops more quickly, a loaded tractor-trailer or a bobtail tractor?","A bobtail tractor can take longer to stop than a loaded tractor-trailer. Bobtail tractors can be very hard to stop smoothly and take longer to stop than a tractor-semitrailer loaded to gross weight.");
Q(14,5,2,"What should you do when your trailer starts to jackknife?","Release the brakes to get traction back",["Brake harder", "Use the trailer hand brake", "Accelerate hard"],"Do not use the trailer hand brake to straighten the rig. Release the brakes so the trailer wheels can grip the road again.");
F(14,5,"What should you do when your trailer starts to jackknife?","Release the brakes to get traction back. Do not use the trailer hand brake to straighten the rig. Release the brakes so the trailer wheels can grip the road again.");
Q(14,5,2,"What is offtracking?","The rear wheels follow a different path than the front wheels in a turn",["The trailer comes loose", "The steering pulls to one side", "Tire tread separates"],"When a vehicle goes around a corner, the rear wheels follow a different path than the front wheels. Longer vehicles offtrack more.");
F(14,5,"What is offtracking?","The rear wheels follow a different path than the front wheels in a turn. When a vehicle goes around a corner, the rear wheels follow a different path than the front wheels. Longer vehicles offtrack more.");
Q(14,5,2,"What is the trailer hand valve (trolley valve) for?","Testing the trailer brakes",["Parking", "Normal stopping while driving", "Releasing the fifth wheel"],"The hand valve should be used only to test the trailer brakes. Never use it for parking or to straighten a skid.");
F(14,5,"What is the trailer hand valve (trolley valve) for?","Testing the trailer brakes. The hand valve should be used only to test the trailer brakes. Never use it for parking or to straighten a skid.");
Q(14,5,2,"What does the tractor protection valve do?","Keeps air in the tractor's brake system if the trailer breaks away or develops a bad leak",["Protects the engine from overheating", "Controls the fifth wheel", "Stops the compressor"],"The tractor protection valve closes automatically when air pressure is low, in the range of 20 to 45 psi.");
F(14,5,"What does the tractor protection valve do?","Keeps air in the tractor's brake system if the trailer breaks away or develops a bad leak. The tractor protection valve closes automatically when air pressure is low, in the range of 20 to 45 psi.");
Q(14,5,2,"What shape and color is the trailer air supply control on newer vehicles?","A red, eight-sided knob",["A yellow diamond knob", "A blue round knob", "A green lever"],"The trailer air supply control is a red eight-sided knob used to control the tractor protection valve.");
F(14,5,"What shape and color is the trailer air supply control on newer vehicles?","A red, eight-sided knob. The trailer air supply control is a red eight-sided knob used to control the tractor protection valve.");
Q(14,5,2,"Which air line is often colored blue?","The service line",["The emergency line", "The supply line", "The fuel line"],"Service lines are often coded blue and emergency (supply) lines red, so they do not get mixed up.");
F(14,5,"Which air line is often colored blue?","The service line. Service lines are often coded blue and emergency (supply) lines red, so they do not get mixed up.");
Q(14,5,2,"Which air line supplies air to the trailer air tanks?","The emergency (supply) line",["The service line", "The return line", "The exhaust line"],"The emergency line supplies air to the trailer tanks and controls the emergency brakes on combination vehicles.");
F(14,5,"Which air line supplies air to the trailer air tanks?","The emergency (supply) line. The emergency line supplies air to the trailer tanks and controls the emergency brakes on combination vehicles.");
Q(14,5,2,"What happens if the emergency air line loses pressure, for example if the trailer breaks loose?","The trailer emergency brakes come on",["Nothing", "The tractor brakes release", "The compressor stops"],"Loss of pressure in the emergency line causes the trailer emergency brakes to come on.");
F(14,5,"What happens if the emergency air line loses pressure, for example if the trailer breaks loose?","The trailer emergency brakes come on. Loss of pressure in the emergency line causes the trailer emergency brakes to come on.");
Q(14,5,2,"Trailers built on or after what date are required to have ABS?","March 1, 1998",["January 1, 1990", "March 1, 2005", "July 1, 2010"],"Trailers and converter dollies built on or after March 1, 1998 are required to have ABS.");
F(14,5,"Trailers built on or after what date are required to have ABS?","March 1, 1998. Trailers and converter dollies built on or after March 1, 1998 are required to have ABS.");
Q(14,5,2,"Where is the ABS malfunction lamp on a trailer?","A yellow lamp on the left side, at the front or rear corner",["A red lamp on the roof", "A blue lamp at the rear door", "Inside the cab only"],"Trailers have yellow ABS malfunction lamps on the left side, either on the front or rear corner.");
F(14,5,"Where is the ABS malfunction lamp on a trailer?","A yellow lamp on the left side, at the front or rear corner. Trailers have yellow ABS malfunction lamps on the left side, either on the front or rear corner.");
Q(14,5,2,"When coupling, how should the trailer height be set?","Slightly low, so the trailer is raised slightly when the tractor backs under it",["As high as possible", "Level with the top of the tractor cab", "Height does not matter"],"The trailer should be low enough that it is raised slightly by the tractor when the tractor is backed under it.");
F(14,5,"When coupling, how should the trailer height be set?","Slightly low, so the trailer is raised slightly when the tractor backs under it. The trailer should be low enough that it is raised slightly by the tractor when the tractor is backed under it.");
Q(14,5,2,"After coupling, how do you check that the connection is secure?","Pull the tractor gently forward with the trailer brakes locked",["Drive off and listen", "Look at the landing gear only", "Honk the horn"],"With the trailer brakes on, pull forward gently to check that the trailer is locked onto the tractor.");
F(14,5,"After coupling, how do you check that the connection is secure?","Pull the tractor gently forward with the trailer brakes locked. With the trailer brakes on, pull forward gently to check that the trailer is locked onto the tractor.");
Q(14,5,2,"When you inspect the coupling, the fifth wheel locking jaws should be closed around","The shank of the kingpin",["The head of the kingpin", "The landing gear", "The air lines"],"Check that the locking jaws have closed around the shank of the kingpin, not the head, and that there is no space between the upper and lower fifth wheel.");
