Q(14,8,2,"Compared with other commercial vehicles, doubles and triples need what?","More following distance and larger gaps",["Less following distance", "Higher speed on curves", "Harder braking"],"Doubles and triples need more space because they cannot be turned or stopped suddenly. Allow more following distance.");
F(14,8,"Compared with other commercial vehicles, doubles and triples need what?","More following distance and larger gaps. Doubles and triples need more space because they cannot be turned or stopped suddenly. Allow more following distance.");
Q(14,9,2,"When is a tank endorsement required for a Class A or B vehicle?","Hauling liquid or liquid gas in tanks over 119 gallons each with 1,000 gallons or more in total",["Only when hauling hazardous materials", "Any tank over 10 gallons", "Only for milk"],"A tank endorsement is needed for tanks with an individual rated capacity of more than 119 gallons and an aggregate rated capacity of 1,000 gallons or more.");
F(14,9,"When is a tank endorsement required for a Class A or B vehicle?","Hauling liquid or liquid gas in tanks over 119 gallons each with 1,000 gallons or more in total. A tank endorsement is needed for tanks with an individual rated capacity of more than 119 gallons and an aggregate rated capacity of 1,000 gallons or more.");
Q(14,9,2,"What is the most important item to check for on any tank vehicle?","Leaks",["Paint condition", "Radio", "Mud flaps"],"On all tank vehicles, the most important item to check for is leaks. Carrying liquids or gases in a leaking tank is a crime.");
F(14,9,"What is the most important item to check for on any tank vehicle?","Leaks. On all tank vehicles, the most important item to check for is leaks. Carrying liquids or gases in a leaking tank is a crime.");
Q(14,9,2,"Why are liquid tankers easy to roll over?","High center of gravity",["Low tire pressure", "Short wheelbase only", "Heavy engines"],"Much of the load's weight is carried high off the road. Tankers can turn over at the speed limits posted for curves.");
F(14,9,"Why are liquid tankers easy to roll over?","High center of gravity. Much of the load's weight is carried high off the road. Tankers can turn over at the speed limits posted for curves.");
Q(14,9,2,"What is liquid surge?","Movement of the liquid in partially filled tanks",["A sudden rise in fuel pressure", "Overfilling a tank", "Engine hesitation"],"Surge results from movement of the liquid in partially filled tanks. On a slippery surface the wave can shove a stopped truck into an intersection.");
F(14,9,"What is liquid surge?","Movement of the liquid in partially filled tanks. Surge results from movement of the liquid in partially filled tanks. On a slippery surface the wave can shove a stopped truck into an intersection.");
Q(14,9,2,"What do baffles in a liquid tank do?","Help control forward and backward surge; side-to-side surge can still occur",["Stop all surge in every direction", "Divide the tank into sealed separate tanks", "Keep the liquid cold"],"Baffled tanks have bulkheads with holes that let liquid flow through. They help control forward and backward surge, but side-to-side surge can still occur.");
F(14,9,"What do baffles in a liquid tank do?","Help control forward and backward surge; side-to-side surge can still occur. Baffled tanks have bulkheads with holes that let liquid flow through. They help control forward and backward surge, but side-to-side surge can still occur.");
Q(14,9,2,"Which tanks usually have no baffles?","Tanks that carry food products such as milk",["Gasoline tanks", "Acid tanks", "Propane tanks"],"Un-baffled (smooth bore) tanks usually transport food products, because sanitation rules forbid baffles that are hard to clean.");
F(14,9,"Which tanks usually have no baffles?","Tanks that carry food products such as milk. Un-baffled (smooth bore) tanks usually transport food products, because sanitation rules forbid baffles that are hard to clean.");
Q(14,9,2,"What is outage?","Room left in a tank for the liquid to expand as it warms",["A power failure in the cab", "An empty compartment", "The legal weight limit"],"Never load a cargo tank totally full. Liquids expand as they warm, and the room you leave is called outage.");
F(14,9,"What is outage?","Room left in a tank for the liquid to expand as it warms. Never load a cargo tank totally full. Liquids expand as they warm, and the room you leave is called outage.");
Q(14,9,2,"Which three things decide how much liquid you may load in a tank?","How much the liquid expands in transit, its weight, and legal weight limits",["Tank color, hose length and road type", "Trip length, weather and fuel price", "Driver age, tire size and speed limit"],"The amount to load depends on the amount the liquid will expand in transit, the weight of the liquid, and legal weight limits.");
F(14,9,"Which three things decide how much liquid you may load in a tank?","How much the liquid expands in transit, its weight, and legal weight limits. The amount to load depends on the amount the liquid will expand in transit, the weight of the liquid, and legal weight limits.");
Q(14,9,2,"How should a tank vehicle take curves and ramps?","Well below the posted speeds",["At the posted speed", "Slightly above the posted speed", "In neutral"],"Take highway curves and on ramp/off ramp curves well below the posted speeds.");
F(14,9,"How should a tank vehicle take curves and ramps?","Well below the posted speeds. Take highway curves and on ramp/off ramp curves well below the posted speeds.");
Q(14,9,2,"Can an empty tank vehicle take longer to stop than a full one?","Yes",["No, never", "Only on ice", "Only downhill"],"Empty tank vehicles may take longer to stop than full ones. Wet roads double the normal stopping distance.");
F(14,9,"Can an empty tank vehicle take longer to stop than a full one?","Yes. Empty tank vehicles may take longer to stop than full ones. Wet roads double the normal stopping distance.");
Q(14,10,2,"How many hazard classes are there for hazardous materials?","Nine",["Five", "Twelve", "Three"],"A material's hazard class reflects the risks associated with it. There are nine different hazard classes.");
